On Oct 1, 2010, at 11:58, Luke Bergen wrote:

> oops.  I thought that's what {du'u} was.  I thought {se du'u} was  
> "the sentence that expresses a bridi".  So I would think that {se  
> du'u} is the actual sentence and {du'u} is the basic relationship  
> that the {se du'u} is expressing.

Yes, exactly so.

1. You said "you are fat" to me    do cusku lu do plana li'u mi
2. You said that I am fat          do cusku lo se du'u mi plana

#1 implies #2; #2 is less specific than #1, in that it does not give  
exact phrasing.

Why do you think you need to use some other abstraction than {du'u}?

